**THE FIVE ITEMS (each an explicit item of READY; the numbering is the gate's). New `it`s carry words, NOT work numbers or shape ids, in titles and comments (the gate counted "ids in titles 0"); the finding ids may appear only in the commit message.**
**1. B648-1 - three scanner shapes hide a later bang (`quoteScan :108-139` feeding `shellCommands :94-96`).** (a) X11: an apostrophe in a trailing comment (`echo hello # it's here`, then the bang command) opens a quote, so the next lines join into one command; bash 3.2.57 and 5.2.15 print `event not found` for the hidden command; zsh does not (comments are off in `zsh -f -i`). (b) X20: an apostrophe in a heredoc body (`cat <<EOF` / `don't` / `EOF`, then the bang command): bash and zsh fail. (c) X14: `echo $'it\'s' "a!b"` (ANSI-C quote honouring `\'`): zsh only. Fix shape (the gate's): outside quotes a `#` at the start of a word ends the scan of that line; a heredoc body (`<<WORD`, `<<-WORD`, quoted word) is not read as shell text; `$'` opens a single-quote span that honours `\'`. **Rows (one `it` each, red BY NAME on the old scanner): X11, X20, X14, each asserting the later bang command is found at its own line number. Over-fix guards (up to three more `it`s, stated before the first run, each with the mutant that turns it red or "green by design"):** a `#` inside a word (`echo a#b "c!d"`) is not a comment and is still found; a `#` inside double quotes stays text; the existing row at `:237` (`echo "a!b" # a trailing comment` found) stays green. **Property (first): no text that the OLD scanner flags and a pty confirms fails (`event not found`) may become unflagged (kill-lost 0).** Prove it: scratch copies of the old and the new file, each with one `export` line added, driven by a throwaway test in `wt-b695-mut` only (never committed), over the shapes X1a X1b X2 X3a-c X11 X14 X19 X20 of the gate's tables plus, for each branch you add, one edge shape (for example a bang in a double-quoted span INSIDE a trailing comment, a heredoc body that holds a double-quoted bang, a `#` after `$'..'`), each measured by a pty in the image's bash 5.2 (`docker run --network none`, a local `python3 -I` pty; zsh if the image has it, else say NOT TESTED). At least these shapes; **any shape where the old scanner flagged and a pty fails but the new does not: that is a STOP with the shape and the table (the gate's kill-lost clause), never a silent change of an old row.** If fixing (a)-(c) forces a trade-off between a hidden later bang and a bang inside the comment or body itself, STOP with ONE question and the pty table.
**2. B648-2 - a fence on the same line as a list marker is not seen (`FENCE_LINE :23`).** `- ```sh` and `1. ```sh` (X1a, X1b; the gate's closing fence is indented) are green on old and new (not a regression); a pty fails on the bang line in bash and zsh; the indented closing fence is then read as an OPENING of a bare block. Fix shape: an optional list marker (`[-*+]`, or digits then `.` or `)`, then spaces) before the marks. Rows: X1a `sectionOf("- ```sh", BAD-with-indent, "  ```")` expects the bang found at its line; X1b the same with `1. `; guard: a prose list item that merely starts with `- ` (no marks) opens nothing.
**3. B648-3 - language tags are compared case-sensitively (`:20`, `:71-72`).** `SH`, `Bash`, `ZSH` (X3a-c) are not scanned; a pty prints `event not found`. Fix shape: lower-case the first word of the info string before the lookup. Rows: `sectionOf("```Bash", BAD, "```")` expects `[3]`, and `SH`, `ZSH` likewise (a loop of `it`s, one per tag); guard: `TEXT` and `Json` stay unscanned.
**4. B648-4 - four weak pins, one row each (`:77`, `:154`, `:130`, `:81`), each non-equivalent and killed by NO current row.** Rows green on the fixed file by design; red under the named mutant (your own text swap in `wt-b695-mut`, anchor count 1, applied to the FINAL scanner: re-measure the anchor text after items 1-3 shift the lines): **M16** delete the `flush();` at the closing-fence branch (`:77`): a block that ends on `\` followed by a block holding a bang command expects the bang's OWN line number (`sectionOf("```sh", "echo a \\", "```", "```sh", BAD, "```")` expects `[6]`; the mutant joins it to line 3). **M26** `command.text.includes("psql")` becomes `includes("p")` (`:154`): `scanDeploy` over a shell block that holds the letter p but no psql (for example `echo preparing`) must still THROW `no psql command` (row 14's block `echo hello` holds no p). **W049** `index += 1` after a backslash outside quotes becomes `index += 2` (`:130`; the anchor needs its enclosing `} else if (char === "\\") {` line, because the double-quote branch also holds `index += 1`): `found('echo a\\ "b!c"')` is true. **W028** `&&` becomes `||` in `inString` (`:81`): a line that starts with `#` right after a `\` continuation (joined text `echo a  # ...`) is a comment and is dropped, not appended; the row pins the scanner's current decision and says so in its comment unless a pty measurement backs a stronger sentence.
**5. B648-5 - the comment at `:346` and the second assertion of row 12 (`:350`) claim more than a shell does.** True on Linux bash 5.2.15 and zsh 5.9, wrong for macOS bash 3.2.57 on a line that starts with `#` (R10b2), and the planted `# not a comment!"` has its bang right before the closing quote, a history event in no shell tested. Fix shape: reword to "...and expands history in the string (bash 3.2 skips a line that starts with #)" and plant `# not a comment !x"`. Proof: the reworded assertion is red under the mutant that drops the `!inString` exemption at `:82` (the `#`-line is then skipped), and a pty in the image's bash 5.2 prints `event not found` for the new plant (quote it; the bash 3.2.57 clause is RELAYED from the gate, say so in the comment's evidence).

